\f1\fs22 Inside, a series of plunge pools were set into the transparent floor, each heated to temperatures calibrated by someone who believed comfort was a character flaw. The water was piped from high-altitude glacial reservoirs and infused with minerals whose names were longer than their health benefits. Bathers could look down at the Layer churning beneath them like a living thing with indigestion. The whole arrangement conveyed the serene confidence of the Airborne class: that even nakedness and vulnerability could be monetised, provided the poor were kept on the other side of the glass.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dunhilton was already in the hottest pool when Gerry arrived, arms spread along the rim, steam rising around him like a man being slowly rendered for stock. A towel attendant in clinical white guided Gerry to a robe and gestured toward the pool. The robe felt like sandpaper and the towel felt like it had come straight from a kiln.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Gerry looked down through the floor. The Layer shifted beneath his feet. He gripped the railing.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Get in, Gerry.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I\'92m fine here, CEO.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Nonsense. It\'92s therapeutic. The minerals are extraordinary. Something volcanic.\'94 Dunhilton smiled the way a crocodile smiles at a drinking hole.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Gerry lowered himself in. The water was punishingly hot. Through the transparent floor beneath him, the Layer swirled and pulsed. He planted his feet firmly on the glass bottom and tried not to think about structural engineering.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dunhilton grew tense; he had a lot riding on the next few minutes. \'93Operation Parramatta,\'94 he said quietly and emphatically. \'93Status update please.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Your Serene Corporateness, we\'92ve recovered the virus.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dunhilton beamed, and exhaled with relief. \'93You mean the Parramatta Virus, yes?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Gerry tried to let it go, but he couldn\'92t. He had family living in Parramatta. \'93I wonder what the good citizens of Parramatta would think of naming a deadly virus after their fair city.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dunhilton looked puzzled. \'93
\f2\i You
\f1\i0  called it that. You said it was a Parramatta virus.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I said it was a 
\f2\i pararetrovirus
\f1\i0 .\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You don\'92t get much more retro than Parramatta.\'94 Dunhilton\'92s eyes narrowed. \'93You know, Gerry, I have your welfare at heart. I care about you.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Yes, CEO.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93And I want to keep caring about caring about you, Gerry. You understand?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Yes, CEO.\'94 Gerry found that all kinds of creepy. The water found new pores to scald.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>An awkward silence fell like a fog on a peat bog. \'93Well, what would 
\f2\i you
\f1\i0  call it?\'94 Dunhilton tested.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Me, your Serene Corporateness?\'94 Gerry shrugged. \'93Perhaps, TMinusVirus? That\'92s what the research called it.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dunhilton expected the mere act of blinking at Gerry would tease out an explanation.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93It\'92s effectively the TPlusVirus\'97\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You mean the Gummy Bear Virus,\'94 Dunhilton said. \'93So you only recovered the Parramatta virus? Not the Gummy Bear Virus as well?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93We only recovered the TMinusVirus,\'94 Gerry said flatly.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93The what?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Gerry reluctantly modified his answer. \'93The Parramatta virus.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93So the Gummy Bear Virus is still contained? In the last lab, under Jamison Tower?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Either there or in an unknown location.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93We must ensure no more labs are unsealed,\'94 Dunhilton ordered.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Arrangements are being made.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Good.\'94 Dunhilton sank a centimetre deeper into the pool, which Gerry interpreted as satisfaction. \'93Did you ever find Hermann and Anstey?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Gerry smiled. \'93As a matter of fact, we did. In the last lab under the Pale.\'94 There was a pause. \'93Dead, of course.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dunhilton exhaled with relief.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93It seems they survived for some time,\'94 Gerry added.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Starvation?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Hard to say. Their corpses looked\'85 well preserved. Sterile environment, we guess, at least before we broke into it.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Holy tax. What did they look like? Zombies? Vampires?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93According to the reports, they looked more like werewolves,\'94 Gerry answered, hoping he could end the conversation quicker by running with it.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Outstanding.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93And their hair was perfect.\'94 Gerry smiled.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dunhilton couldn\'92t tell if he was being baited, so he let it slide. The floor beneath the pool gave a faint creak. Gerry\'92s knuckles whitened on the rim.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Settle down, Gerry. It\'92s rated for twice this weight.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Comforting, CEO.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93So how long before we can deploy the Parramatta Virus against the Elvi?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>This was the question Gerry was loath to answer. \'93We\'92ve recovered a great deal of documentation; there is much to digest. The research team estimates between two and three months.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Three months?\'94 Dunhilton blustered. \'93We haven\'92t got that kind of time. The Elvi are united and armed, Gerry.\'94 Dunhilton took a breath. \'93And they\'92re getting 
\f2\i wealthy
\f1\i0 , Gerry, with all those record sales.\'94\

\f2\i <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Ah, there it is
\f1\i0 , Gerry thought.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93We can\'92t have wealthy Cloudborne, especially not clones,\'94 Dunhilton blustered on. \'93It would be the end of civilised society as we know and live it, Gerry.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Yes, CEO. When all is done and dusted, no one will cry over a few dead hospitality clones.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Exactly right, Gerry. No one misses a broken dishwasher or espresso machine. They just throw them out and buy new ones, better ones. It\'92s good for the economy, Gerry, that\'92s what it is.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The steam rose between them. Below the glass, the Layer rolled over like a cat stretching in its sleep.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Indeed, your Serene Corporateness.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93So understand when I say, three months is too long to wait to clean the mice out of the grain silo.\'94 Dunhilton\'92s face of consternation transformed into that of a smiling Gaboon viper. \'93I\'92m depending on you, Gerry. You understand that\'85\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Have patience, CEO. I will have it ready to deploy come mid-November.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I want it by Melbourne Cup Day, Gerry. No one will notice extra drug-related deaths for at least a week after that.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Gerry swallowed his objections. He knew if there weren\'92t Elvi dying by the Cadillac-load by the appointed time, he\'92d be dead instead. \'93The first Tuesday in November it is, your Serene Corporateness,\'94 he said, his neck so taut, he sounded like he might be being strangled.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dunhilton smiled and lowered himself until the water lapped at his chin. \'93Relax, Gerry. Enjoy the view.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Gerry looked down through the glass. The Layer stared back.
